  description="A decentralized OPA bundle distribution system delivering sub-millisecond authorization to 30+ microservices on the FICO Platform."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Every microservice on the platform constantly has to answer one question: is
    this user allowed to do this action on this thing? The obvious architecture
    is a central authorization server that every service calls on every request.
    We deliberately did not build that, for two reasons. It is slow, because it
    adds a network round-trip to every single request. And it is a single point
    of failure: if the central server degrades, nothing on the platform can
    authorize anyone, and the whole platform effectively stops. For a platform
    serving enterprise financial institutions under strict uptime SLAs, that
    blast radius was unacceptable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The useful mental model is a guest list photocopied to every door. Instead of
    every bouncer radioing a central office to ask whether someone is on the
    list, each door holds its own current copy of the list and the rules, and
    refreshes it every ten to twenty seconds. Decisions are instant and local.
    The honest cost: if you remove someone from the master list, the doors do
    not know until their next refresh. That staleness window is the central
    trade-off of the whole design, and it is examined below rather than hidden.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    This was a team system, and the scope split matters. The decentralized
    bundle architecture was a platform-level design I worked within. My hands
    were on three parts: I designed and built the
    <a href="/work/kubernetes-rbac-operator/">Kubernetes operator</a> that
    provisions authorization data declaratively (it gets its own page), I
    contributed to the bundle build pipeline that packages policy and data into
    distributable artifacts, and I built the shared Java client library that
    services include to enforce authorization, including its dual-sidecar
    bootstrap logic. The parts I did not build, like the policy authoring API, I
    worked with closely and understand deeply, but I will not claim I wrote
    their internals.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    Authorization data (roles, permissions, groups, and their relationships) is
    authored through a central gRPC API and stored relationally. From there, the
    system is a pipeline: changes publish an event to a message bus, a build
    step wakes up, queries the current data, pulls the Rego policy logic, and
    packages both into a compressed OPA bundle pushed to object storage. Every
    service pod runs an OPA sidecar that polls for new bundles every ten to
    twenty seconds, using ETags so an unchanged bundle costs a "not modified"
    response instead of a re-download.
  </p>

