  <p>
    On a live request, the calling user's signed JWT carries their groups. The
    service asks its local sidecar, which walks groups to roles to permissions
    to the specific resource entirely in memory and returns allow or deny in
    under a millisecond. No network call, no dependency on any central service
    being up. If every central component went down at once, services would keep
    authorizing correctly with the bundles they already hold.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>

  <Tradeoff decision="Decentralized policy bundles over a central authorization server">
    <span slot="bought">
      Sub-millisecond local evaluation with no per-request network hop, and
      survival of control-plane outages: sidecars keep serving decisions from
      their cached bundles.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Eventual consistency. A revoked permission takes one build-and-poll cycle
      (seconds to a couple of minutes) to propagate. We judged that acceptable
      because RBAC changes are planned administrative actions, and for a genuine
      emergency (a compromised credential) short JWT expiry is the real kill
      switch, not bundle propagation.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Event-driven bundle builds over building inline on each write">
    <span slot="bought">
      Writes stay fast, because a permission change never blocks on the slow
      work of querying everything and packaging a bundle, and the message bus
      provides retries and at-least-once delivery for free.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      More moving infrastructure between a write and its effect, and the
      freshest bundle is always one asynchronous build behind the latest write,
      which widens the consistency window the previous trade-off already
      accepts.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Generating two bundle formats simultaneously over a big-bang format migration">
    <span slot="bought">
      Every consumer migrates from the old flat format to the newer multi-tenant
      format on its own schedule, with nothing breaking on cutover day.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Every change builds two bundles and the team maintains two policy files,
      doubling the surface area that has to stay correct until the last consumer
      migrates.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="A dual-sidecar bootstrap over blocking startup until the first bundle loads">
    <span slot="bought">
      The authorization system can authorize its own cold start. A second
      sidecar with a known-good dataset baked into its image answers until the
      primary loads its first live bundle, which took cold-start authorization
      failures to zero.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Availability over strict consistency during the startup window: the
      baked-in snapshot can be slightly staler than live data for the first few
      seconds. The full reasoning is in
      <a href="/writing/opa-dual-sidecar-bootstrap/">its own write-up</a>.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Adopted as the authorization standard across the platform: any service
      adds the shared client library and gets policy-based access control with
      no central dependency.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sub-millisecond policy evaluation on the authorization path of a platform
      processing 1M+ daily authenticated requests at a 99.98% uptime SLA.
    </li>
    <li>
      30+ microservices consume the client library; provisioning for 20+ of
      them runs through the declarative operator.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
  <p>
    I would stand up a policy test harness on day one. The Rego policies are the
    component on the critical path of every single request, and in any system
    the code that makes every allow-or-deny decision deserves the deepest test
    investment, before any of the surrounding plumbing. I would also invest
    earlier in measuring end-to-end propagation latency, from write to sidecar,
    because the eventual-consistency window is the design's one honest weakness
    and you should know its real size, not its theoretical one.
  </p>

